  "worked_solution": "To plot $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14244490/assets/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{4}{5}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14244490/assets/ea9bd36b532b46d3edcc99497b77e75288499a4b02239f5d2416cff6a4259478.png)\n\n- Translate it by $3$ units up to get the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14244490/assets/e6798ba049ab7040eec51d2da525d3383816578423e11384ced1f3665a2e50fd.png)",
